+The arithmetic against this convention remains severe. For a feasible uncapped fixed point, nominal SINR is approximately `Γ(n)`. At occupancy one, `Γ(1)` is the table floor, so `qΓ(1)` falls below every mode when the de-rating exceeds the negligible numerical clearance. The reported 9.16e−9 dB headroom cannot accommodate a 1.1–4.8 dB de-rating. The policy can therefore spend energy on a singleton while selecting no mode. Its universal singleton rejection need not have been anticipated merely because its constituent rules were deliberate.
+
+I tried to turn that pathology into a contradiction of radio physics; that attack fails. The occupancy comparison changes transmit power. A heavily occupied beam can receive much more power, including forced cap power when its target exceeds the table, and decode while the singleton does not. This is possible under the declared controller. Calling it “physically impossible” confuses a control policy with propagation at fixed power. Conversely, the claim that conservative mode selection “buys nothing” fails: demotion can buy decoding robustness at fixed power, until it runs out of modes.
+
+The old declaration's own argument that compensation buys no reserve is also incomplete. Keeping `q` in selection while solving against `Γ/q` buys nominal reserve above the retained target mode. That is a coherent alternative. Algebra alone cannot choose between conserving power through rate demotion and spending more power to preserve the target mode. The scientific obligation now is to disclose that the old occupancy-activation mechanism depends on this policy. The attempted absolute veto therefore fails: versioning can legitimately change the policy. What survives is the requirement to justify that choice explicitly, rather than presenting zero attainment as proof of a breached service contract or an overlooked implementation instruction.
+
+**A2. More power makes the network worse — `CONSTRAINS_IT`.**
+
+The adverse mechanism is real. With assignments fixed, larger SINR targets increase coupled power demand; aggressors increase interference, formerly feasible links can hit their caps, and some users lose service. The [Claude fixed-assignment panel](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/LADDER-CLAUDE-2026-09-10.md) reports decoded instances falling from 24,351 to 22,046 at occupancy three, 30,800 to 24,561 at four, and 9,029 to 7,619 at five. These are meaningful counterexamples to a universal user benefit, although that diagnostic variant has the numerical limitation discussed in A7.
+
+The claim that *every* user's radiated power rises by exactly 1.107–4.830 dB is inaccurate. That range describes the imposed SINR-target margin `−10 log10(q)`. For two uncapped solutions with unchanged geometry and assignment,
+
+`p'_i/p_i = (1/q_i) × (N_i + I'_i)/(N_i + I_i)`.
+
+Interference feedback can amplify the increase; caps compress it; already forced-cap transmissions may not change; reoptimising assignments changes occupancy and the comparison itself. The nonlinear PA supply law also means energy does not scale directly with RF power.
+
+I checked the stronger claim that these costs outweigh service and efficiency gains across the reported widths. It fails on the available aggregate results. In the [five-width report](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/BEAMWIDTH-MARGIN-2026-09-10.md:29), every B/U/J cell has higher pooled EE, more served user-steps and nonzero target attainment under the simple margin variant. The old rule has zero target attainment in every cell. B/U/J denote baseline, terminal unilateral search and bounded joint search; all counts below are out of 800 user-steps per arm.
+
+| One-sided width | Old served B/U/J | Margin served B/U/J | Margin target B/U/J | Margin J/U EE gap |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 1.66° | 313 / 779 / 790 | 452 / 800 / 800 | 174 / 550 / 541 | −0.303% |
+| 2.40° | 334 / 761 / 774 | 413 / 800 / 800 | 131 / 487 / 474 | +1.209% |
+| 3.32° | 203 / 701 / 729 | 230 / 800 / 800 | 60 / 443 / 434 | +0.985% |
+| 4.50° | 56 / 528 / 607 | 77 / 781 / 781 | 24 / 338 / 337 | +3.205% |
+| 6.65° | 19 / 158 / 220 | 28 / 694 / 722 | 9 / 258 / 269 | +7.315% |
+
+These are the published **simple-division** diagnostics, not a five-width validation of the final clearance variant. On the twenty-anchor final variant, U energy rises from 58.33 to 73.07 kJ while bits rise from 1,660.78 to 3,194.99 Gbit and pooled EE from 28.47 to 43.73 Mbit/J. Thus even the version with clearance contradicts aggregate deterioration on its panel. [Twenty-anchor comparison](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/LADDER-CODEX-2026-09-10.md:11).
+
+Aggregate improvement does not establish Pareto improvement, fairness, improved availability for every user or a deployable policy benefit. Nor should J's advantage over U be conflated with the provisioning comparison: J attains fewer targets than U at the first four widths. These distinctions constrain claims; they do not sustain “the margin rule makes the whole measured network worse.”
+
+**A3. An outcome-selected correction-and-width pair — `CONSTRAINS_IT`; a bundled promotion of a favorable width is unsupported.**
+
+The strongest allegation is that the project removes the mechanism producing its headline, then changes antenna design until coordination is positive again. The numerical temptation is unmistakable. Moving from 1.66° to 3.32° in the published margin sweep restores a positive J/U gap while reducing U/J target attainment by 107 user-steps each and baseline service from 452 to 230. At 6.65°, the gap reaches +7.315%, but U/J target counts fall to 258/269 and service to 694/722. Increasing a relative coordination benefit while degrading the underlying service is not an outcome-independent reason to choose an antenna.
+
+I tested whether both changes were invented together to rescue C3. That accusation is not established. The [commissioning log](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/chain.log:194) starts the width investigation before the ladder adjudications. The source-convention discrepancy independently justified an audit, and both beam prompts require every point and prohibit selecting a winner. The reports retained negative results.
+
+However, “the source requires the wider beam” is unavailable as an automatic justification. The [owner's later ruling](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/codex-beamwidth.md:39) explicitly makes beam width a chosen system-design parameter. The false source-attribution comment requires correction; the numerical width does not thereby require doubling. Furthermore, [v1.7 §4](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-PHYSICS-SUCCESSOR-PRIORITY-DECLARATION-v1.7-ERRATUM-2026-09-09.md:8) preserves the original primary result and permits only separately labelled regime findings after its failure. It supplies no permission to replace the primary with a favorable observed width.
+
+Provisioning must therefore be adjudicated at the existing 1.66° one-sided width. A later antenna choice needs a separate design rationale that would choose the same width if C3's sign were unfavorable. All observed widths remain disclosed development sensitivities. Losing the old headline is evidence against simple gain-maximising selection of provisioning alone, but it does not immunise a subsequent package from outcome selection. The eight-anchor −0.303% and twenty-anchor +0.513% also concern different panels; A7 further separates their implementations.
+
+**A4. Three sources do not provide three independent validations — `CONSTRAINS_IT`.**
+
+This attack succeeds against the claimed independence. The [Codex commission](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/codex-ladderfloor.md:12) supplies the mechanism and asks to “Confirm from the code.” Claude's ladder commission is more open, but shares controller, code and data family. The literature task receives the controller's description of the alleged mismatch and a selected twelve-paper corpus, including seven mandatory papers. The launcher identifies that literature reader as the same Claude model family used for the other review. These are correlated investigations with different checks, not independent physical replications.
+
+The stronger allegation that Claude echoed Codex's completed report fails the chronology: Codex began at 16:53 and finished at 18:03; Claude began at 17:24 and finished at 17:47. This does not establish blinding to intermediate findings or framing, but it prevents treating the final Codex document as Claude's demonstrated source. [Commissioning record](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/chain.log:223).
+
+The [literature report's own limitations](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/LIT-PHYSICS-2026-09-10.md:286) weaken its categorical conclusion. Its corpus lacks a discrete MODCOD rate model, an availability-to-margin mapping and discussion of receiver implementation margin. Absence of this exact convention from twelve selected papers does not establish that no coherent design uses it, or uniquely validate `Γ/q`. I audited that report's scope and provenance; I did not independently reread the twelve PDFs.
+
+Total dismissal of the evidence also fails. The inspectable inequality `qΓ(1) < τ_min`, the reproduced original outputs and scalar/dense bit accounting do not depend on a vote among language models. They establish the behavior. Agreement about whether that behavior warrants replacing the declared policy carries much less evidential weight. This adjudication and its delegated readings are likewise not an additional independent experimental replication.
+
+**A5. Recomputing the project is too expensive; a narrower remedy suffices — `CONSTRAINS_IT`.**
+
+The replacement cost extends far beyond changing two provisioning sites and their dense twins. Every successor result depending on RF power, coupled interference, caps, modes, bits, energy, service, occupancy or EE requires regeneration. Search paths and catalogues change, so old selected assignments cannot simply be rescored and called new optima. Physics-dependent observations, forecasts and C1/C2/C3 labels need regeneration; affected learned heads need retraining; learned trajectories, contrasts, uncertainty intervals and admission decisions need new evaluation. Calibration diagnostics must be recomputed while keeping the frozen price for this comparison; changing the price would introduce another model decision.
+
+“Every existing figure is invalid” is nevertheless too broad. Old figures remain records of the old model. Unchanged TLE identities, geometry and exogenous fading tapes can be retained, as can demonstrably unaffected controls and figures. Their reuse needs a dependency or parity check. What fails is carrying an old-model figure into a successor claim without recomputing its affected inputs.
+
+The available cost evidence supports a small diagnostic comparison, not a budget assurance for the entire successor:
+
+| Scope | Existing measurement or explicit estimate |
+|---|---|
+| Twenty-anchor comparison | Harness reports 1,141 s for cold original evaluation and 713 s for margin evaluation with reusable worlds but new solves; estimated cold margin time 813 s. |
+| Exact label generation | 4,402 paired C1/C2 targets over five anchors took 591 s. Linear scaling to 180 anchors is approximately 5.9 serial wall-hours for these labels alone. |
+| Earlier formal primary projection | 8.94 process-core-hours against a 160-core-hour budget, from an old-rule rehearsal; this excludes a demonstrated full successor training and confirmation cost. |
+| Planned learned evaluation | Six arms × 16 seeds × two worlds/date × approximately 160 dates × approximately 30 steps gives roughly 921,600 arm-world-step endpoints at one setting. This is planned scope, not completed work or a runtime estimate. |
+
+Sources: [harness timings and cache limits](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-harness-ws/EVAL-HARNESS-2026-09-10.md:255), [label rebuild](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/RETRAIN-RESULT-2026-09-09.md:135), [formal rehearsal](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-ENGINE-STAGE4H-REPORT-2026-09-09.md), [six-arm contract](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/V025-STAGES-6-8-CONTRACT-v1.1-AMENDMENT-2026-09-09.md:14). Cached identical-result reads are not reruns, and changed physics invalidates anchor-result caches.
+
+I cannot sustain “the project cannot afford even this comparison.” I can sustain “full replacement affordability has not been established.” The narrower scientific remedy is substantial: retain the declared primary, disclose its policy-induced floor, report rate attainment, and publish both rules with the collapse of the coordination mechanism. That answers whether the old result is robust to the provisioning convention. Merely adding a footnote while retaining a general physical consolidation claim would not suffice. A different deployed service objective could justify a successor, but the present diagnostic purpose does not require replacing the primary to be fulfilled.
+
+**A6. Zero attainment is an instrumentation artefact — `FAILS`.**
+
+I tested missing or miscopied flags, bit/Mbit units, the integration horizon, threshold rounding, duplicate TDM discounts, target-mode rather than transmitted-mode credit, and contamination by per-user energy attribution.
+
+Arithmetic over the hash-verified [24,000-row receipt](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/.scratch/beamwidth/per-user-metric.json) reproduces every target flag using `bits >= 50,000,000 × (47 × 0.640) = 1,504,000,000 bits`, with zero mismatches. All 12,000 original-rule rows fail. The largest original average rate is 44.67646 Mbit/s; even a 30-second divisor leaves it below 45 Mbit/s. This is not a near-equality measurement accident.
+
+The [dense endpoint](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/batch.py:403) credits transmitted-mode SE only after decoding, applies the TDM fraction once and integrates per-user bits. The [separate scalar reconstruction](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/.scratch/beamwidth/per_user_metric.py:137) uses the same physical quantities. Energy attribution is downstream of the bits-based attainment flag. At uncapped nominal equality, selecting below the lowest-threshold mode meeting the required SE cannot meet that SE; the reported original census finds insufficient SE in all 846,846 decoded attempts.
+
+There is a real service-counter discrepancy: scalar service uses AND across a user's transmissions within a boundary, while dense service uses OR. The ladder report discloses it. On the original matched eight anchors, scalar B/U/J counts are 301/778/789 and published dense counts 313/779/790. It changes the meaning of “served,” but not integrated bits or target attainment. The published positive-bit definition reproduces every receipt row. Preserve that definition for the comparison and disclose the discrepancy; do not silently repair the guard while changing provisioning.
+
+These checks establish the zero as a consequence of this model and accounting convention. They do not transform a nominal target into a guaranteed-service contract.
+
+**A7. The advertised successor is not one numerical implementation — `BLOCKS_THE_CHANGE` as a ready-to-authorise, fully evidenced implementation.**
+
+This attack finds a material inconsistency. The final twenty-anchor result includes a bounded refinement that verifies both nominal `Γ/q` clearance and quantile-adjusted target-mode clearance. Simple division plus the inherited solver had left 1,179 uncapped occupancy-one no-mode attempts only 5.76e−12–2.616e−9 dB below threshold. Refinement changed the reported twenty-anchor J/U gap from +0.544766% to +0.512537%, with selectors reoptimised. [Ladder disclosure](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/LADDER-CODEX-2026-09-10.md:71).
+
+The [beam implementation](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/.scratch/beamwidth/margin_batch.py:18) only divides the target; it lacks the [ladder's additional clearance loop](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/.scratch/ladder-floor-20260910/cleared_margin_batch.py:394). Matching existing receipt IDs supplies a comparison without running an experiment:
+
+| Same eight anchors, 1.66° one-sided | U EE, Mbit/J | J EE, Mbit/J | J/U gap | Target B/U/J |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| Simple division, published beam sweep | 42.29790 | 42.16984 | −0.302750% | 174 / 550 / 541 |
+| Additional strict clearance, existing ladder subset | 43.35918 | 43.28252 | −0.176800% | 174 / 540 / 539 |
+
+The simple ladder and beam assignments match 24/24; strict-clearance assignments match the beam in only 18/24. Dense served counts remain 452/800/800. Evidence: [simple results](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/.scratch/ladder-floor-20260910/margin-result.json), [strict results](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/.scratch/ladder-floor-20260910/margin-cleared-result.json), [strict per-user census](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/.scratch/ladder-floor-20260910/scalar-census-summary-cleared.json), and [beam receipt](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/.scratch/beamwidth/margin-sweep-one-sided-1.66.json).
+
+Thus the attempted rescue of a positive narrow-width sign fails: the available strict subset is still negative. But −0.303% is not the strict implementation's matched estimate, and the complete five-width curve has not been established for that implementation. Constants and iteration budget can remain unchanged while numerical clearance changes discrete choices. Its exact algorithm, certificates and scalar/dense behavior must be part of the reviewed successor specification. The all-ones identity check establishes original-rule reproduction; it does not establish correct target clearance when the divisor is `q`.
+
+**A8. Availability, causality and generality are overstated — `CONSTRAINS_IT`.**
+
+I tested the claim that retaining the target mode establishes 90% availability. At an uncapped corrected nominal fixed point, with desired-link fading factor `F_d`,
+
+`SINR_real = Γ × (F_d/q) × (N + I_nominal)/(N + I_realised)`.
+
+Consequently target-mode decoding requires `F_d >= q × (N + I_realised)/(N + I_nominal)`, rather than simply `F_d >= q`. The code uses separate nominal and realised interference. A finite-sample, elevation-binned desired-fade quantile does not guarantee a percentile of coupled SINR, network availability or 50-Mbit/s endpoint delivery. Target-mode selection itself is conditional on feasibility and numerical clearance. The remaining strict-variant target failures already demonstrate the limit.
+
+I also checked the counterfactual summaries. The 66.38% restoration is panel-wide; occupancy-one restoration after removing de-rating is 74.02%. The converged relaxed-cap control uses 100 W, rather than a literally absent cap; one configuration is invalid at 10⁹ W. The singleton failure survives the valid relaxed-cap control and the algebra, so those corrections do not rescue the old singleton policy. The quoted “cap from occupancy ten rather than twelve” is not universal: the Claude fixture binds at eleven rather than thirteen. Cap onset depends on geometry and coupling. The reported restoration differences should not be treated as a unique additive causal decomposition: interventions change coupled powers and their effects can overlap. [Counterfactual and fixture evidence](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/LADDER-CLAUDE-2026-09-10.md).
+
+Finally, 2,000 means twenty user-anchor populations of 100, not 2,000 independent people; the 12,000 sweep results reuse users, steps and arms across five widths. Transmission counts additionally repeat boundaries and slot subdivisions. They are census denominators, not independent samples establishing narrow confidence bounds. The searches maximise fixed-price `B − η_ref E` at boundary zero and commit across 48 boundaries, with a bounded joint catalogue and no deployment deadline in these diagnostics. A positive selection certificate need not imply a positive committed EE gap. The [per-user metric report](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/PER-USER-METRIC-2026-09-10.md) preserves the pooled gap's sign under both energy allocations, so changing attribution does not rescue the negative narrow-width result. None of these diagnostics establishes a population-wide, learned or deployable coordination claim.
+
+If the change proceeds, preserve the original declared primary, seals, source, receipts, figures and unfavorable findings as results of the nominal-power convention, and explicitly state that the successor reverses v1.9's deliberate policy to pursue target-mode selection at the existing design percentile; disclose that all reported outcomes were known before this decision and make the rationale independent of C3's sign. Freeze the existing 1.66° one-sided beam width, constants, thresholds, signs, seeds, horizons, price, service guard and acceptance rules for the provisioning comparison; give any antenna change its own design decision, retain every already observed width as exploratory, and never promote a positive width to erase the original primary result. Specify and review the numerical clearance algorithm at every affected scalar and dense site, then regenerate all affected power/interference solves, search paths, catalogues, service and rate metrics, EE figures, calibration diagnostics at the frozen price, features, labels, learned models, trajectories, contrasts, intervals and admission decisions before carrying them into successor claims; reuse only demonstrably unaffected exogenous inputs and controls. Publish both rules, all widths, energy costs, user losses, caps, failures and negative signs, including the simple-versus-strict numerical distinction, and report the current full-width strict comparison as uncompleted until it exists. Disclose the shared commissioning and assumptions of the reviews, the nominal/full-buffer service semantics, the scalar/dense service discrepancy, repeated-observation denominators, TRAIN-only panels and bounded-search limitations; claim neither 90% network availability nor guaranteed target delivery from the quantile identity. Any later confirmation must follow a prospectively frozen protocol on the reserved unused confirmation data, with unchanged acceptance criteria and failures reported in full.

